NNAAMMEE
       lslogins - display information about known users in the system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llssllooggiinnss [options] [--ss|--uu[=_U_I_D]] [--gg _g_r_o_u_p_s] [--ll _l_o_g_i_n_s] [_u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Examine the wtmp and btmp logs, _/_e_t_c_/_s_h_a_d_o_w (if necessary) and _/_p_a_s_s_w_d
       and output the desired data.

       The optional argument _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e forces llssllooggiinnss to print all available
       details about the specified user only. In this case the output format
       is different than in case of --ll or --gg and unknown is _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e reported
       as an error.

       The default action is to list info about all the users in the system.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options
       too.

       --aa, ----aacccc--eexxppiirraattiioonn
           Display data about the date of last password change and the account
           expiration date (see sshhaaddooww(5) for more info). (Requires root
           privileges.)

       ----bbttmmpp--ffiillee _p_a_t_h
           Alternate path for btmp.

       --cc, ----ccoolloonn--sseeppaarraattee
           Separate info about each user with a colon instead of a newline.

       --ee, ----eexxppoorrtt
           Output data in the format of NAME=VALUE. See also option ----sshheellll.

       --ff, ----ffaaiilleedd
           Display data about the users' last failed login attempts.

       --GG, ----ssuupppp--ggrroouuppss
           Show information about supplementary groups.

       --gg, ----ggrroouuppss=_g_r_o_u_p_s
           Only show data of users belonging to _g_r_o_u_p_s. More than one group
           may be specified; the list has to be comma-separated. Unknown group
           names are ignored.

           Note that the relation between user and group may be invisible for
           the primary group if the user is not explicitly specified as group
           member (e.g., in _/_e_t_c_/_g_r_o_u_p). If the command llssllooggiinnss scans for
           groups then it uses the groups database only, and the user database
           with primary GID is not used at all.

       --LL, ----llaasstt
           Display data containing information about the users' last login
           sessions.

       --ll, ----llooggiinnss=_l_o_g_i_n_s
           Only show data of users with a login specified in _l_o_g_i_n_s (user
           names or user IDs). More than one login may be specified; the list
           has to be comma-separated. Unknown login names are ignored.

       --nn, ----nneewwlliinnee
           Display each piece of information on a separate line.

       ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       ----nnoottrruunnccaattee
           Don’t truncate output.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Specify which output columns to print. The default list of columns
           may be extended if _l_i_s_t is specified in the format _+_l_i_s_t.

       ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output all available columns. ----hheellpp to get a list of all supported
           columns.

       --pp, ----ppwwdd
           Display information related to login by password (see also --aaffLL).

       --rr, ----rraaww
           Raw output (no columnation).

       --ss, ----ssyysstteemm--aaccccss
           Show system accounts. These are by default all accounts with a UID
           between 101 and 999 (inclusive), with the exception of either
           nobody or nfsnobody (UID 65534). This hardcoded default may be
           overwritten by parameters SSYYSS__UUIIDD__MMIINN and SSYYSS__UUIIDD__MMAAXX in the file
           _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_g_i_n_._d_e_f_s.

       ----ttiimmee--ffoorrmmaatt _t_y_p_e
           Display dates in short, full or iso format. The default is short,
           this time format is designed to be space efficient and human
           readable.

       --uu, ----uusseerr--aaccccss
           Show user accounts. These are by default all accounts with UID
           above 1000 (inclusive), with the exception of either nobody or
           nfsnobody (UID 65534). This hardcoded default maybe overwritten by
           parameters UID_MIN and UID_MAX in the file _/_e_t_c_/_l_o_g_i_n_._d_e_f_s.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
